## Opening Treasure Chests

A treasure chest can contain four types of loot. Each has a different gold value when appraised.

### The Loot Enum

```xiom
enum Loot {
  Gold(amount: Int),
  Gem(name: Str),
  Potion(effect: Str),
  Junk,
}
```

### Creating Loot

```xiom
let chest1 = Loot.Gold(50);
let chest2 = Loot.Gem("Ruby");
let chest3 = Loot.Potion("Healing");
let chest4 = Loot.Junk;
```

### Appraising Loot Value

The gold value depends on the loot type. Gems are always valuable. Potions are moderate. Junk is worthless.

```xiom
fn Loot.value(self) -> Int {
  match self {
    Gold(amount) => { return amount; }
    Gem(name) => {
      if name == "Ruby" { return 200; }
      if name == "Diamond" { return 500; }
      return 100;
    }
    Potion(effect) => {
      if effect == "Healing" { return 30; }
      if effect == "Strength" { return 50; }
      return 20;
    }
    Junk => { return 0; }
  }
}
```

### Describing Loot

```xiom
fn Loot.describe(self) -> Str {
  match self {
    Gold(amount) => { return amount + " gold coins"; }
    Gem(name) => { return "A sparkling " + name; }
    Potion(effect) => { return "A potion of " + effect; }
    Junk => { return "Some old junk"; }
  }
}
```

### Opening Chests

```xiom
chest1.value();    // 50
chest2.value();    // 200
chest3.value();    // 30
chest4.value();    // 0

chest2.describe(); // "A sparkling Ruby"
```

